                            <comment id="15163165" author="corneadoug" created="Wed, 24 Feb 2016 15:13:32 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Some possible reads about it that give us 2 solutions for that issue:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Change shortcuts in general:&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20040329-00/?p=40003&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20040329-00/?p=40003&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Make Profiles:&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/adobe/brackets/issues/8100&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://github.com/adobe/brackets/issues/8100&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Key remapping would probably too much of a hassle to do for anybody, so either we change the shortcuts (but we might have them similar to other software on purpose), or we create different shortcut profiles based on languages.&lt;br/&gt;
However, not everybody an an instance of Zeppelin might use the same keyboard profile, so config doesn&apos;t make since at instance level.&lt;br/&gt;
Either we successfully implement, a keyboard detection on webapp side, or we should be able to change the profile from the UI (and save those preferences in a cookie or something)&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15168897" author="andarin" created="Fri, 26 Feb 2016 12:30:18 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;+1 I&apos;m typing on a German keyboard, and it&apos;s the same problem - I cannot type square brackets. I could see how only AltGr could active 3rd level (like &quot;&lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;&quot;), but Crtl+Alt is received as something else (like in Linux). However I wondered if a minor feature like changing node width should overwrite such a common shortcut (for every non-English keyboard).&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15169191" author="moon" created="Fri, 26 Feb 2016 15:34:47 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Then how about Ctrl+Alt+&apos;&lt;del&gt;&apos; and Ctrl+Alt+&apos;=&apos; for reduce width end enlarge width? (both &apos;&lt;/del&gt;&apos;, &apos;=&apos; in english keyboard)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&apos;-&apos;, &apos;=&apos; key often used to change size of font in web browser with other key combinations.&lt;br/&gt;
while &apos;&lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;&amp;#39;, &amp;#39;&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;&apos; key often used to change tabs in many applications with other key combinations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So i think it make more sense to change width with &apos;-&apos;, &apos;=&apos; and use &apos;&lt;span class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;&amp;#91;&amp;#39;, &amp;#39;&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;&apos; for the other purpose (like move paragraph focus).&lt;br/&gt;
What do you think?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of course, long term solution would be let each user set who&apos;s own shortcut.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15171630" author="andarin" created="Mon, 29 Feb 2016 09:25:30 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Both in German and in French keyboard, there are important 3rd layer keys on English &quot;=&quot; and &quot;-&quot; (i.e. &quot; \ &quot; and &quot; ] } &quot;). See layouts in the wiki:&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/KB_Germany.svg/550px-KB_Germany.svg.png&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;German&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c4/French_pc_keyboard.svg&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;French&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You could keep current layout if you distinguish between &quot;Ctrl + Alt&quot; and &quot;AltGr&quot; (the latter for 3rd keys in German/French), or if you use something like Ctrl + Shift + &quot; = &quot; or Ctrl + Shift + &quot; - &quot; as shortcuts in Zeppelin .&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15173290" author="moon" created="Tue, 1 Mar 2016 06:14:05 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;It looks like browser treats AltGr as Ctrl = true and Alt = true.&lt;br/&gt;
So, there&apos;s no way to distinguish &quot;Ctrl + Alt&quot; and &quot;AltGr&quot;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ctrl + Shift + &quot; = &quot; or Ctrl + Shift + &quot; - &quot;  fine as an alternative.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15173634" author="andarin" created="Tue, 1 Mar 2016 11:32:20 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Great. Thank you very much!&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
